---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ImageTool from '../components/ImageTool.tsx';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'Resize Image to 100 KB Online | SizeTune';
const description =
  'Shrink a photo to 100 KB or less in your browser without uploading it. See what a 100 KB limit allows, how to keep faces sharp, and how to check the file.';
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune: resize image to 100 KB', '/resize-image-to-100kb/', description);
---
<Base pageType="tool" width="wide" title={title} description={description} path="/resize-image-to-100kb/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Hero title="Resize an image to 100 KB" lead="The tool below starts with a 100 KB maximum already selected. Add your picture, look at the preview and download the result. If your form names a different limit, or a minimum as well, change the numbers in the tool." />

  <ImageTool client:load defaultMaxKb={100} />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-4">
      This tool works inside your browser and cannot run while JavaScript is off. Turn it on and reload to resize your
      photo to 100 KB. The notes below about what a 100 KB file can hold are still useful with any other editor.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div class={`mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-10 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>What a 100 KB limit gives you</h2>
    <p>
      One hundred kilobytes is roomy compared with the tiny limits some forms use, yet it is still far smaller than a
      modern phone photo, which is usually several megabytes. Most of that gap is closed by two things: fewer pixels and
      a sensible JPEG quality. Because the budget is generous, you rarely need to give up much. A portrait cropped to
      the head and shoulders can stay clear enough to read a face, a name badge and a plain background.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page is a good starting point when a form says "up to 100 KB" for a passport-style photo, an identity card
      scan or an uploaded certificate. Check your own form for the exact maximum, the file types it accepts and any
      pixel dimensions, because those details differ from one form to the next and we do not list them here.
    </p>

    <h2>Why a bigger budget still needs care</h2>
    <p>
      It is tempting to assume that 100 KB means the tool can ignore detail. It cannot. A full-frame 12-megapixel photo
      squeezed into 100 KB would have to be compressed hard, and the result looks smeared. A better route is to keep
      the quality reasonable and let the picture become smaller in pixels. A face that fills the frame at 600 by 800
      pixels looks better at 100 KB than the same face lost inside a 4,000 pixel wide scene.
    </p>
    <p>
      SizeTune aims below your maximum counted as 1,000 bytes per KB, so a result passes even when a portal counts 1 KB
      as 1,024 bytes. The result is shown in 1,024-byte KB, which is why a 100 KB target is displayed as up to about
      97.7 KB. The small gap is the safety margin.
    </p>

    <h2>Steps that keep a 100 KB photo sharp</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Start from the camera original.</strong> A photo that arrived through a chat app has already been
        shrunk once. Compressing it again adds visible blockiness for no gain in size.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Crop before you shrink.</strong> Remove sky, walls and empty margins in your phone's editor. Every
        pixel you drop is a pixel you do not have to compress.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Use quality-first when pixels are fixed.</strong> If the form states a width and height, type them in
        and let the tool vary only the compression.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Use dimensions-first when only KB is stated.</strong> Letting the picture shrink usually keeps edges
        cleaner than pushing quality to the floor.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Zoom into the preview.</strong> Check eyes, glasses frames, text on clothing and the line where hair
        meets the background. Those places show damage first.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Photos, scans and screenshots behave differently</h2>
    <p>
      A portrait photo has soft gradients that JPEG handles well, so 100 KB is usually comfortable. A scan of a
      printed certificate is different: it has fine black text on white paper, and aggressive compression leaves a
      grey haze around each letter. For documents, keep more pixels and accept a lower quality number, and check that
      the smallest text is still readable. A screenshot with flat colour is often tiny as a PNG already, so converting
      it to JPEG only helps if the form requires that format.
    </p>

    <h2>When your form also sets a minimum</h2>
    <p>
      Some forms want a file between, say, 50 and 100 KB. Enter both numbers in the range fields. If your picture ends
      up below the minimum, the tool raises the JPEG quality to store more detail. A small, plain image may still fall
      short, and the tool says so rather than adding filler bytes that a portal would strip on its next save. For a
      photo that is already tiny, going back to a larger original is the honest fix. If you need a smaller target, the{' '}
      <a href="/resize-image-to-20kb/">20 KB page</a> covers what changes at that scale, and the{' '}
      <a href="/resize-signature-to-10kb/">10 KB signature page</a> covers handwriting.
    </p>

    <h2>Check the file before you upload</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Read the size and dimensions displayed in the result panel.</li>
      <li>Open the downloaded file's properties on your device to confirm the size.</li>
      <li>Make sure the extension matches the format your form accepts, such as .jpg.</li>
      <li>Upload the file as it was saved, without forwarding it through a messaging app first.</li>
      <li>Look at the portal's own preview after the upload and go back if it looks soft.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For a plain-language explanation of pixels, kilobytes and formats, read the{' '}
      <a href="/guides/photo-size-for-online-forms/">photo size guide</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about 100 KB photos</h2>
    <h3>Is 100 KB enough for a clear passport-style photo?</h3>
    <p>
      Usually yes, if the picture is cropped to the head and shoulders and not larger in pixels than the form needs.
      Busy backgrounds and very high pixel counts are what push a file past the limit.
    </p>
    <h3>What pixel size usually fits in 100 KB?</h3>
    <p>
      For a cropped portrait, somewhere in the hundreds of pixels on each side is typical, but it depends on the
      picture. Read the dimensions the tool shows rather than relying on a rule of thumb.
    </p>
    <h3>Can I use a scan of a certificate at 100 KB?</h3>
    <p>
      Often, but text is less forgiving than faces. Keep enough pixels for the smallest print to stay legible and check
      the preview closely before you download.
    </p>
    <h3>Can I resize without losing any quality?</h3>
    <p>
      No. Meeting a byte limit means discarding some information. The aim is to discard what you are least likely to
      notice, and the preview lets you judge the result before you download.
    </p>
    <h3>Does my photo leave my device?</h3>
    <p>
      No. The resizing runs in your browser. Read the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for details, or return to
      the <a href="/">SizeTune home page</a> for the general tool.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
